# Nickel-Based Superalloys for Advanced Turbine Engines: Chemistry, Microstructure, and Properties

Tresa M. Pollock (University of Michigan) and Sammy Tin (University of Cambridge / IIT), Journal of Propulsion and Power 22(2):361-374, 2006. DOI: 10.2514/1.18239.

NOTE: Body below is assembled from structured excerpts captured via an alternate extractor because the University of Michigan DeepBlue PDF returned HTTP 403 to both the pipeline fetcher and curl. Excerpts cover all major sections (constitution, processing, properties); some connective text is missing.

## Abstract
The chemical, physical, and mechanical characteristics of nickel-based superalloys are reviewed with emphasis on the use of this class of materials within turbine engines. The role of major and minor alloying additions in multicomponent commercial cast and wrought superalloys is discussed. Microstructural stability and phases observed during processing and in subsequent elevated-temperature service are summarized. Processing paths and recent advances in processing are addressed. Mechanical properties and deformation mechanisms are reviewed, including tensile properties, creep, fatigue, and cyclic crack growth.

## Superalloys in Gas-Turbine Engines
Nickel-based superalloys typically constitute 40-50% of the total weight of an aircraft engine and are used most extensively in the combustor and turbine sections of the engine where elevated temperatures are maintained during operation. Structural components such as engine cases are also fabricated by investment casting; turbine disks via wrought/powder processing.

## Constitution
Although FCC nickel is the major constituent, many alloys contain up to 40 wt% of a combination of five to ten other elements. The Ni-Al system is the binary basis: aluminum added to gamma-Ni forms the gamma-prime phase (nominal Ni3Al), an ordered intermetallic with L12 crystal structure. "The presence of a high-volume fraction of the gamma-prime phase is key to strengthening." Refractory alloying elements with large differences in electronic structure and atomic radii compared to Ni — Mo, W, Nb, and Re — are added for solid-solution strengthening of the gamma matrix. Minor additions of B, C, Hf, and Zr form borides or carbides, often at grain boundaries.

Phase-partitioning data for Rene N5 (turbine-blade alloy) and IN100 (disk alloy): Re, Mo, Cr, and Co preferentially partition to the matrix gamma phase; Ti, Ta, and Al partition to the gamma-prime precipitate phase.

Ni-based superalloys containing elevated refractory levels are prone to precipitation of topologically close-packed (TCP) phases at elevated temperature.

Table 1 compositions (wt%, bal. Ni), single-crystal generations:
- First-generation SX: PWA 1480 (Cr 10, Co 5, W 4, Ta 12, Al 5, Ti 1.5), Rene N4, CMSX-3 (Cr 8, Co 5, Mo 0.6, W 8, Ta 6, Al 5.6, Ti 1)
- Second-generation SX (3% Re): PWA 1484 (Cr 5, Co 10, Mo 2, W 6, Ta 9, Re 3, Al 5.6), Rene N5 (Re 3, Al 6.2), CMSX-4 (Cr 6.5, Co 9, Mo 0.6, W 6, Ta 6.5, Re 3, Al 5.6, Ti 1)
- Third-generation SX (5-7% Re): Rene N6 (Cr 4.2, Co 12.5, Mo 1.4, W 6, Ta 7.2, Re 5.4, Al 5.8), CMSX-10 (Cr 2, Co 3, Mo 0.4, W 5, ...)

## Processing
Directional solidification into columnar-grained or single-crystal forms by withdrawing an investment mold downward through a radiation baffle from a high-temperature furnace at thermal gradients G = 10-100 C/cm and withdrawal rates R = 5-40 cm/hr (Bridgman process; liquid-metal-cooled LMC variant raises cooling rate). Chemistry-sensitive grain defects include freckles and misoriented grains. Powder-metallurgy routes exist for disk alloys where refractory levels exceed conventional castability. Forging/cogging occur at ~1000 C due to high intrinsic strength.

## Properties
Yield strengths often 900-1300 MPa and ultimate tensile strengths 1200-1600 MPa at room temperature. Turbine-disk alloys designed for service below ~800 C (overspeed burst protection); cast blade alloys experience temperatures up to 1100 C whereas disk alloys are typically limited to less than 700 C.

