# Resource-Efficient Manufacturing Technology for Titanium Aluminide Aerospace Components

Bünck, Salber, Stoyanov (ACCESS e.V. / MTU Aero Engines context), Transactions of the Indian National Academy of Engineering (2023). DOI: 10.1007/s41403-023-00436-5. Open access (CC-BY), publishedVersion.

NOTE: Body below is assembled from structured excerpts captured via an alternate extractor because Springer and the RWTH repository served JavaScript bot challenges to both the pipeline fetcher and curl. Excerpts cover abstract, introduction, methods, results, and conclusion; some connective text is missing.

## Abstract
Titanium aluminide (TiAl) has been identified as a key material for achieving significant emission reductions in aircraft jet engines due to its high-temperature properties combined with its very low weight compared with conventional nickel-based alloys. Well-known engine manufacturers, therefore, introduced TiAl low-pressure turbine blades in their engines. To enter the market, all manufacturers initially relied on enormous machining allowances for safety reasons, thus accepting a poor buy-to-fly ratio: MTU Aero Engines AG used the casting/forging route with the alloy TNM [Ti–43.5Al–4Nb–1Mo–0.1B (at.-%)] for the highly stressed GTF (geared turbofan), Safran used machining of the [billet] ... ACCESS has, therefore, developed an economical TiAl investment casting process to industrial maturity, realizing a minimum machining allowance of 0.7 mm in a process-safe manner. In a study, it was evaluated that carbide cutters with AlTiN coating are excellently suited for machining TiAl.

## Introduction (excerpts)
- Titanium aluminide is an intermetallic compound which, due to its high-temperature properties (oxidation resistance and strength retention at high temperatures, approx. 750–800 °C), is used primarily for aircraft engines.
- Processability is very demanding due to brittle room temperature properties. TiAl alloys are correspondingly expensive, but offer the enormous advantage of low weight, which is only half that of nickel-based alloys.
- As a result, TiAl can substitute nickel-based alloys in applications up to about 800 °C (hence primarily in low-pressure turbines).
- The most common alloys today are GE 48-2-2 for casting and TNM for forging.
- Production approaches worldwide: [investment] casting, forging, cutting/machining (MTU for Geared Turbofan), investment casting with large machining allowance plus machining to final size (GE); GE is the only OEM using printed (3D-printed / additive) TiAl blades.
- PCC (Precision Castparts Corp.) uses massive overstocking followed by mechanical machining in the USA.
- Developments: "the use of the material TNM in MTU's GTF has been suspended." High potential for TiAl in future engines remains; broader use requires manufacturing cost reduction.
- Economically, investment casting is the most suitable method since it allows very small machining allowances, saving expensive material and reducing machining effort.

## Methods/Experimental (excerpts)
- Ball nose cutter with internal cooling proved most suitable for machining the gas path of engine blades; shank diameter 12 mm, length 82 mm.
- All blades investigated were cast from GE48-2-2 type alloy manufactured industrially to aerospace specifications by GfE Gesellschaft für Elektrometallurgie mbH, Nuremberg.
- Recycled-material study: gravity casts into a metal die at recycled-to-primary ratios in 2

