--- /tmp/opencode/e2e-light/research/notes/final_report_jet-engine-turbine-metals-a1b636.md
+++ /tmp/opencode/e2e-light/research/notes/final_report_jet-engine-turbine-metals-a1b636.md
@@ -3,9 +3,9 @@
 Modern jet engine turbine blades are made overwhelmingly of **nickel-based superalloys**, cast as **single crystals** and loaded with refractory metals such as rhenium, tungsten, tantalum and molybdenum. In the cooler rear (low-pressure) stages of the newest engines, many blades have switched to **titanium aluminide (γ-TiAl)** intermetallics, while **cobalt-based superalloys** hold their long-standing place among vanes, nozzles and stationary hot-section hardware. No metal alone explains how these parts survive: the alloy's metallurgy buys roughly 1,000–1,100 °C of metal capability, and engineering — internal cooling channels, film cooling and ceramic thermal barrier coatings — does the rest, letting the metal sit safely below its melting point inside gas streams far above it.
 
 ## 1. The Thermal Problem: What Turbine Blades Endure
 
-A jet engine is more efficient the hotter it runs, because higher turbine entry temperature raises the thermodynamic efficiency of the Brayton cycle [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]]. That incentive has pushed gas temperatures at the turbine inlet to roughly 1,400–1,500 °C in flight engines — with the gas leaving the combustor quoted near 3,000 °F (about 1,650 °C) in Pratt & Whitney's landmark account of the single-crystal blade [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]] [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]].
+A jet engine is more efficient the hotter it runs, because higher turbine entry temperature raises the thermodynamic efficiency of the Brayton cycle [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]]. That incentive has pushed gas temperatures at the turbine inlet to roughly 1,400–1,500 °C in flight engines. Pratt & Whitney's landmark account of the single-crystal blade quotes combustor-exit gas near 3,000 °F (about 1,650 °C) [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]] [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]].
 
 The paradox is immediate: nickel-based superalloys begin to melt somewhere between about 2,300 and 2,500 °F (roughly 1,260–1,370 °C) [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]], and even well below that they lose strength rapidly. Left unprotected, a blade in that gas stream would creep — deform slowly and permanently under load — and then fracture. Creep has been called "an insidious life limiter" in the engineering literature [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]].
 
 And the mechanical side is just as brutal. A high-pressure turbine blade spins at thousands of rpm; the centrifugal pull across its span reaches some 20,000 times gravity [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]]. Teaching material from Cambridge puts typical blade-metal conditions near 1,000 °C under roughly 100 MPa of sustained stress — conditions under which most metals creep catastrophically [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]]. The whole machine exists in a narrow window: run hotter for efficiency, keep the metal below the threshold where it dies.

