 ### The γ′ precipitate: strength that grows with temperature
 
 The central trick of the nickel superalloy is the gamma-prime phase, γ′ — an ordered intermetallic, nominally Ni₃(Al,Ti), that precipitates coherently within the FCC nickel matrix [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]]. Modern blade alloys pack in enormous volume fractions — around 70% in the most highly strengthened compositions [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]]; Pratt & Whitney's pioneering Alloy 454 was designed around maximizing a fine γ′ fraction near two-thirds of the material [[the-development-of-single-crystal-superalloy-turbine-blades-gell-duhl-giamei-sup]].
 
-Two properties make γ′ special. First, its flow stress *increases* with temperature up to about 650 °C — the opposite of ordinary metals — so the strengthening phase is at its best exactly where the blade runs hottest [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]]. Second, because γ′ is crystallographically ordered, dislocations cannot glide through it freely: entering the ordered lattice creates an energetically costly anti-phase boundary, so dislocation pairs must force their way through together, slowing plastic deformation dramatically [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]]. This is precipitation hardening engineered at the atomic scale, and it is why yield strengths of 900–1,300 MPa survive at temperatures where steel would sag [[nickel-based-superalloys-for-advanced-turbine-engines-pollock-tin-j-propulsion-a]].
+Two properties make γ′ special. First, its flow stress *increases* with temperature up to about 650 °C — the opposite of ordinary metals — so the strengthening phase is at its best exactly where the blade runs hottest [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]]. Second, because γ′ is crystallographically ordered, dislocations cannot glide through it freely. Entering the ordered lattice creates an energetically costly anti-phase boundary, so dislocation pairs must force their way through together, slowing plastic deformation dramatically [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]]. This is precipitation hardening engineered at the atomic scale, and it is why yield strengths of 900–1,300 MPa survive at temperatures where steel would sag [[nickel-based-superalloys-for-advanced-turbine-engines-pollock-tin-j-propulsion-a]].
 
 ### Refractory solid-solution strengthening — and its limits
 
 Dissolved into the nickel matrix, large, slow-diffusing atoms — molybdenum, tungsten, niobium and above all rhenium — stiffen the γ phase and, critically, slow the diffusion that drives creep [[nickel-based-superalloys-for-advanced-turbine-engines-pollock-tin-j-propulsion-a]]. Rhenium is the generational lever: each step up in Re content buys meaningful additional creep-rupture capability at extreme temperature [[cmsx-4-plus-single-crystal-alloy-development-wahl-harris-superalloys-2016]].

