 ### Killing the grain boundaries: directional solidification and single crystals
 
 Creep loves grain boundaries — they are fast diffusion highways and void-nucleation sites [[designing-for-creep-resistance-nickel-based-superalloys]]. The industry's answer was to remove them progressively. Directionally solidified (DS) castings align columnar grains along the blade's stress axis, eliminating the transverse boundaries where creep damage starts; single crystals go further and eliminate every boundary. DS blades entered military engines in 1969 and commercial service in 1974; single-crystal blades reached production on the JT9D by 1980 [[the-development-of-single-crystal-superalloy-turbine-blades-gell-duhl-giamei-sup]] [[single-crystal-turbine-blade-asme-historic-engineering-landmark-brochure-pratt-w]].
 
-Going single-crystal did more than delete weak planes — it changed alloying logic entirely. With no grain boundaries to strengthen, designers dropped boron, carbon, hafnium and zirconium (the grain-boundary strengtheners) and used that compositional headroom to raise the solution-treatment temperature, dissolve everything, and re-precipitate the finest possible γ′ — a redesign that bought roughly 25 °C of capability over the best DS alloy, worth about threefold creep life [[the-development-of-single-crystal-superalloy-turbine-blades-gell-duhl-giamei-sup]]. Underneath all of this sits the choice of base metal itself: nickel's FCC lattice is close-packed and diffuses slowly at high homologous temperature, which is precisely why nickel, not iron, anchors the family [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]].
+Going single-crystal did more than delete weak planes — it changed alloying logic entirely. With no grain boundaries to strengthen, designers dropped boron, carbon, hafnium and zirconium (the grain-boundary strengtheners) and used that compositional headroom to raise the solution-treatment temperature, dissolve everything, and re-precipitate the finest possible γ′. This redesign bought roughly 25 °C of capability over the best DS alloy, worth about threefold creep life [[the-development-of-single-crystal-superalloy-turbine-blades-gell-duhl-giamei-sup]]. Underneath all of this sits the choice of base metal itself: nickel's FCC lattice is close-packed and diffuses slowly at high homologous temperature, which is precisely why nickel, not iron, anchors the family [[superalloys-a-primer-and-history]].
 
 ## 4. Engineering Heat Away: Coatings, Cooling, and Casting
 
 Metallurgy alone never closes the gap between a ~1,450 °C gas stream and a ~1,350 °C incipient melting point. The rest of the answer is thermal engineering layered onto the metal.

