 Three successor technologies frame what comes next:
 
 - **Ceramic matrix composites (CMCs)** — silicon-carbide fibers in a silicon-carbide matrix — run several hundred degrees hotter than metals at one-third the density, cutting turbine component weight by more than half and enabling up to 2% fuel-burn reduction [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]]. They entered wide commercial service in the LEAP engine's high-pressure turbine shroud in 2016, and the GE9X carries multiple CMC hot-section parts — though in today's engines they occupy static roles (liners, nozzles, shrouds), while rotating blade rows remain metallic, because spinning ceramics must yet prove themselves under extreme centrifugal load and heat together [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]].
-- **Refractory high-entropy alloys (RHEAs)** promise strength well beyond the nickel solvus ceiling, but their standing drawbacks are bluntly stated in the literature: low-temperature brittleness and poor creep strength at elevated temperature [[intrinsic-factors-responsible-for-brittle-versus-ductile-nature-of-refractory-hi]], compounded by oxidation problems — pest oxidation, scale spallation — inherent to refractory metals [[current-status-of-research-on-the-oxidation-behavior-of-refractory-high-entropy]]. They are candidates, not products.
+- **Refractory high-entropy alloys (RHEAs)** promise strength well beyond the nickel solvus ceiling, but their standing drawbacks are bluntly stated in the literature: low-temperature brittleness and poor creep strength at elevated temperature [[intrinsic-factors-responsible-for-brittle-versus-ductile-nature-of-refractory-hi]]. These drawbacks are compounded by oxidation problems — pest oxidation, scale spallation — inherent to refractory metals [[current-status-of-research-on-the-oxidation-behavior-of-refractory-high-entropy]]. They are candidates, not products.
 - **More TiAl, everywhere**: with manufacturing routes maturing and cost falling, titanium aluminides are expected to spread beyond the last LPT stages, trading nickel mass for efficiency wherever temperatures stay under ~800 °C [[resource-efficient-manufacturing-technology-for-titanium-aluminide-aerospace-com]].
 
 Which metals? Rhenium-bearing single-crystal nickel superalloys in the hot rotating stages, cobalt-based superalloys across the stationary hot section, titanium aluminides in the cooler rear stages. Why do they tolerate the heat? Because a coherent ordered precipitate (γ′) gives the alloy strength that *grows* with temperature; because refractory solutes like rhenium strangle the diffusion that drives creep; because single-crystal casting removed the defects creep needs; and because engineers wrapped it all in cooling air and ceramic armor — so the metal itself never has to face the fire alone.

