 ## 5. Limits Today and What Comes Next
 
-The current ceiling is real. Cast blade alloys serve up to about 1,100 °C metal temperature [[nickel-based-superalloys-for-advanced-turbine-engines-pollock-tin-j-propulsion-a]]. Life is brutally sensitive at the margin: a second-generation single-crystal alloy (DD5) held at 1,100–1,150 °C without load for as little as 100 hours lost roughly 40–50% of its subsequent rupture life at service-like conditions — even though its microstructure showed no obvious TCP degradation [[effect-of-overtemperature-exposure-on-the-microstructure-and-mechanical-properti]]. Meanwhile the rhenium that buys high-temperature strength is scarce, heavy in the alloy's final density, and costly — the explicit motivations for reduced-Re designs like CMSX-4 Plus [[cmsx-4-plus-single-crystal-alloy-development-wahl-harris-superalloys-2016]].
+The current ceiling is real. Cast blade alloys serve up to about 1,100 °C metal temperature [[nickel-based-superalloys-for-advanced-turbine-engines-pollock-tin-j-propulsion-a]]. Life is brutally sensitive at the margin: a second-generation single-crystal alloy (DD5) held at 1,100–1,150 °C without load for as little as 100 hours lost roughly 40–50% of its subsequent rupture life at service-like conditions. Yet its microstructure showed no obvious TCP degradation [[effect-of-overtemperature-exposure-on-the-microstructure-and-mechanical-properti]]. Meanwhile the rhenium that buys high-temperature strength is scarce, heavy in the alloy's final density, and costly — the explicit motivations for reduced-Re designs like CMSX-4 Plus [[cmsx-4-plus-single-crystal-alloy-development-wahl-harris-superalloys-2016]].
 
 Three successor technologies frame what comes next:
 
 - **Ceramic matrix composites (CMCs)** — silicon-carbide fibers in a silicon-carbide matrix — run several hundred degrees hotter than metals at one-third the density, cutting turbine component weight by more than half and enabling up to 2% fuel-burn reduction [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]]. They entered wide commercial service in the LEAP engine's high-pressure turbine shroud in 2016, and the GE9X carries multiple CMC hot-section parts. In today's engines, however, CMCs occupy static roles (liners, nozzles, shrouds); rotating blade rows remain metallic, because spinning ceramics must yet prove themselves under extreme centrifugal load and heat together [[ceramic-matrix-composites-taking-flight-at-ge-aviation-steibel-acers-bulletin-ap]].

